50. The deeper unification: intelligence as substrate allocation
TTT, latent reasoning, external memory, tool use, and auxiliary representation learning initially look unrelated.
They can instead be viewed as answers to one question:
\[
\boxed{
\text{where should this information or computation live?}
}
\]
Examples:

static phrase-level association \(\rightarrow\) lookup memory;
general reusable linguistic or reasoning prior \(\rightarrow\) slow weights;
current-request evidence selection \(\rightarrow\) activations;
multi-step internal search \(\rightarrow\) recurrent/looped latent state;
long-context adaptation \(\rightarrow\) fast weights or persistent request state;
fresh external facts \(\rightarrow\) retrieval/tool;
auditable multi-stage control \(\rightarrow\) explicit harness.

The central architecture problem becomes:
\[
\boxed{
\text{Can a learned controller route computation and learning
to the cheapest sufficient substrate?}
}
\]
This extends the earlier idea of credit routing.
Credit routing asks:
\[
\text{where should learning go?}
\]
Compute routing asks:
\[
\text{where should inference happen?}
\]
A mature continually intelligent system needs both.

52. Revised thesis: progressive compilation is also progressive internalization
The original thesis of this note was:
\[
\text{explicit + plastic}
\rightarrow
\text{implicit + efficient}.
\]
The new evidence makes this more concrete.
There are at least three forms of internalization:
\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{memory internalization}
&:
\text{episode/retrieval}
\rightarrow
\text{weights},\\
\text{policy internalization}
&:
\text{harness rule}
\rightarrow
\text{learned controller},\\
\text{computation internalization}
&:
\text{explicit reasoning/action}
\rightarrow
\text{latent representation}.
\end{aligned}
}
\]
Engram shows that some computation should move out of dense neural processing and into lookup.
Coconut and recurrent-depth models show that some reasoning can move out of language and into continuous latent state.
Representation auxiliary learning shows that some pipeline decisions can move out of separate models or generated tokens and into one forward pass.
TTT shows that some context can move out of explicit token storage and into adaptive parameter state.
These are not contradictions. They express the same optimization principle:
\[
\boxed{
\text{place each computation in the substrate
with the best trade-off among capacity, latency, plasticity,
attribution, persistence, and control}.
}
\]
That may be the more general architecture problem underneath continual intelligence.

3. Top-down and bottom-up DP

Top-down: memoized recursion
from functools import cache

def solve(nums):
    n = len(nums)

    @cache
    def dp(i):
        if i &gt;= n:
            return 0

        skip = dp(i + 1)
        take = nums[i] + dp(i + 2)

        return max(skip, take)

    return dp(0)
Advantages:

closest to mathematical recurrence;
often easiest to derive correctly;
computes only reachable states.

Disadvantages:

recursion overhead;
stack-depth limits;
state transitions can be less explicit.



Bottom-up: tabulation
def solve(nums):
    n = len(nums)
    dp = [0] * (n + 2)

    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
        dp[i] = max(
            dp[i + 1],
            nums[i] + dp[i + 2],
        )

    return dp[0]
Advantages:

explicit dependency order;
easy space optimization;
usually less overhead.

9. DFS from first principles
DFS answers a different fundamental question:

What happens if I completely explore one branch before exploring its siblings?

It is useful for:

reachability;
connected components;
cycle detection;
topological ordering;
tree recursion;
enumerating paths;
backtracking.


Recursive template
def dfs(u):
    visited.add(u)

    for v in graph[u]:
        if v not in visited:
            dfs(v)

10. Cycle Detection: Three-color DFS
For directed graphs, use:
0 = unseen
1 = currently in recursion stack
2 = completely processed
A transition
u -&gt; v
where v is color 1 means a directed cycle.
def has_cycle(graph):
    n = len(graph)
    color = [0] * n

    def dfs(u):
        color[u] = 1

        for v in graph[u]:
            if color[v] == 1:
                return True

            if color[v] == 0 and dfs(v):
                return True

        color[u] = 2
        return False

    return any(color[u] == 0 and dfs(u) for u in range(n))
Why color 1 matters:
A visited node is not necessarily evidence of a cycle.
Only an edge back into the current unresolved dependency chain creates a cycle.

Undirected cycle detection with DFS
For an undirected graph, track the node we came from:
def has_cycle(graph):
    n = len(graph)
    visited = [False] * n

    def dfs(u, parent):
        visited[u] = True

        for v in graph[u]:
            if not visited[v]:
                if dfs(v, u):
                    return True
            elif v != parent:
                return True

        return False

    return any(
        not visited[u] and dfs(u, -1)
        for u in range(n)
    )

17. Negative-cycle detection
After V-1 rounds, perform one more relaxation pass.
If some distance can still improve:
\[
dist[u]+w(u,v)&lt;dist[v],
\]
then a reachable negative cycle exists.
Why?
Any cycle-free path contains at most V-1 edges.
An improving path with at least V edges must repeat a vertex.
If repeating that cycle reduces cost, the cycle has negative total weight.
from collections import deque

# normal Bellman-Ford
for _ in range(V - 1):
    changed = False
    for u, v, w in edges:
        if dist[u] != INF and dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]:
            dist[v] = dist[u] + w
            changed = True
    if not changed:
        break

# detect negative-cycle influence
q = deque()
neg = [False] * V

for u, v, w in edges:
    if dist[u] != INF and dist[u] + w &lt; dist[v]:
        if not neg[v]:
            neg[v] = True
            q.append(v)

# propagate to everything reachable from those vertices
while q:
    u = q.popleft()
    for v, _ in graph[u]:
        if not neg[v]:
            neg[v] = True
            q.append(v)

for v in range(V):
    if neg[v]:
        dist[v] = float("-inf")

